gle.</p><p><strong>Greg Dulcich</strong> has room to work \u2014 CIN allow <strong>46 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Greg Dulcich</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 CIN are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.47 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>In the red zone CIN throw it (64% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Ja'Marr Chase</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><p>In the red zone MIA throw it (55% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Jalen Tolbert</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to CIN \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Chase Brown</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><h3>CIN \u2014 a <em>pass-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>CIN stay aggressive through the air even with a lead \u2014 they pass <strong>49%</strong> on early downs when ahead versus the league's 44%. They don't sit on the ball, which keeps their receivers live in scripts where most offenses would be running out the clock.</p><p><strong>Chase Brown</strong> (13 carries, 56 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though CIN's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Ja'Marr Chase</strong> projects for <strong>8 targets and 71 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><h3>MIA \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>MIA are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 MIA project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>De'Von Acha